# Tutorial 05 — Transitions and Effects
# How to use fades, crossfades, screen flashes, and other visual effects between scenes.

@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_05_transitions
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
{{BG_START}}[inc tutorial_count]
[textspeed 28]
[autodelay 1650]
[show lavender center happy]
[wait 200]
Lavender: Time to make things look {i}cinematic{/i}.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Transitions control how one visual state flows into the next — fades, crossfades, flashes, and more.
Lavender: Used well, they're invisible. Used poorly, they pull the player right out of the story.

@label lesson_menu
[show lavender far_left talking]
Lavender: What would you like to learn about?
> Fade to black and back -> section_fade
> Crossfade between backgrounds -> section_crossfade
> Screen flash and shake -> section_screen_fx
> Timing and feel -> section_timing
> Quick reference card -> reference_sheet
> Practice scene -> practice_scene
> Back to Tutorial Hub -> return_hub

# ─── Fade ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_fade
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: The most common transition is a simple {b}fade{/b}. The screen goes dark, then the new scene appears.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[transition fade 450]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[bg classroom]{/color}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: The number is the duration in milliseconds. 450 is snappy — good for location changes within the same scene.
Lavender: For a longer, more dramatic shift — like a time skip — try 800 or even 1200.
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Fades always go through black. They signal a clear break — "we're somewhere else now."
Lavender: If the change is subtler, like day turning to evening in the same location, a crossfade usually works better.
> Next: Crossfade -> section_crossfade
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Crossfade ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_crossfade
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: A {b}crossfade{/b} blends one background directly into another — no black in between.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[transition crossfade 700 field_evening]{/color}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: The old image dissolves as the new one fades in. It feels like time passing, not a hard cut.
Lavender: Great for: day-to-night changes, dream sequences, or gentle scene shifts within the same area.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: The duration matters a lot here. 400–600ms feels brisk and modern. 1000+ feels dreamy and slow.
Lavender: Experiment with the numbers — crossfade speed sets the emotional tone of the transition itself.
> Next: Screen effects -> section_screen_fx
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Screen FX ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_screen_fx
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Beyond transitions, the engine supports screen-level effects for punctuation — flashes and shakes.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: A {b}screen flash{/b} is a quick burst of color — usually white, for lightning, explosions, or magical impacts.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[screen flash 0.22 120 1 1 1]{/color}
narrator: The numbers: intensity, duration (ms), and RGB color (1 1 1 = white).
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: A {b}screen shake{/b} rattles the whole view — for impacts, earthquakes, or shock moments.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[screen shake 0.3 200]{/color}
narrator: Intensity and duration. Keep it short — a long shake gets annoying fast.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: These are exclamation marks. One per scene is powerful. Three per scene is exhausting. Use them deliberately.
> Next: Timing -> section_timing
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Timing ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_timing
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: The biggest mistake people make with transitions? Wrong duration for the moment.
Lavender: Here's a rough guide:
narrator: {b}200–350ms{/b} — snappy cut, almost instant. Action scenes, rapid dialogue shifts.
narrator: {b}400–600ms{/b} — standard transition. Location changes, scene breaks.
narrator: {b}700–1000ms{/b} — slow and deliberate. Emotional moments, time skips.
narrator: {b}1200ms+{/b} — dramatic. Opening/closing scenes, major story beats.
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: And always pair transitions with {b}[wait]{/b} when you need the player to sit with the effect:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[transition fade 800]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[wait 400]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[bg new_scene]{/color}
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: That extra 400ms of darkness after the fade? It creates anticipation. The player wonders what's coming next.
> Reference card -> reference_sheet
> Try it yourself -> practice_scene
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Reference Card ──────────────────────────────────────────────

@label reference_sheet
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Transition and effects cheat sheet:
narrator: {b}[transition fade N]{/b} — fade to black over N ms, then show new content.
narrator: {b}[transition crossfade N id]{/b} — dissolve directly to background "id" over N ms.
narrator: {b}[screen flash intensity duration R G B]{/b} — brief color flash.
narrator: {b}[screen shake intensity duration]{/b} — camera shake effect.
narrator: {b}Timing guide:{/b} 200–350ms snappy, 400–600ms standard, 700–1000ms dramatic, 1200ms+ cinematic.
narrator: {b}Combine with [wait]{/b} — add pauses before or after transitions for impact.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Less is more. A well-timed fade says more than five flashy effects in a row.
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Practice Scene ──────────────────────────────────────────────

@label practice_scene
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Let's see transitions in context. Pick a scenario:
> A quiet scene change -> practice_quiet
> A dramatic reveal -> practice_dramatic
> An action beat -> practice_action

@label practice_quiet
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Let's move somewhere new. Close your eyes...
[transition fade 600]
[wait 300]
{{BG_CROSSFADE}}
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: ...and we're here. A smooth fade — no drama, just a gentle shift.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: 600ms fade with a 300ms hold in the dark. Clean and unobtrusive.
[set practice_result 1]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_dramatic
[show lavender center emphasis]
Lavender: Wait — do you feel that?
[wait 500]
[transition fade 1000]
[wait 600]
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: A long fade, held in darkness. The player doesn't know what's on the other side.
Lavender: That uncertainty is the whole point. Slow transitions create suspense.
[set practice_result 2]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_action
[show lavender center emphasis]
Lavender: Look out!
[screen shake 0.4 180]
[wait 100]
[screen flash 0.3 100 1 1 1]
[show lavender right emphasis]
narrator: The ground trembled. A crack of light split the sky.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Shake plus flash, back to back. Short and punchy — the player barely has time to process it.
[set practice_result 3]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_wrap
[show lavender center happy]
[if practice_result == 1]
Lavender: Quiet transitions are the workhorse. You'll use them hundreds of times — make sure they feel right.
[elif practice_result == 2]
Lavender: Dramatic transitions earn their impact by being rare. Save them for moments that matter.
[else]
Lavender: Action effects are fun to write but easy to overdo. One good shake is worth ten mediocre ones.
[endif]
Lavender: Try changing the durations in this file and see how it changes the feel. Milliseconds matter!
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

@label return_hub
[goto {{HUB_TARGET}}:topics_page_2]
